1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:10,144
We have an adder with a register on its feedback wire,

2
00:00:10,144 --> 00:00:16,258
and we know that a register needs its input steady in a window around each rising edge.

3
00:00:16,358 --> 00:00:22,142
Today we put the two together and ask how fast this circuit can be clocked.

4
00:00:23,383 --> 00:00:25,355
Give everything a number.

5
00:00:25,355 --> 00:00:32,589
The register has a clock to q delay of one nanosecond and a setup time of one nanosecond.

6
00:00:32,689 --> 00:00:38,037
The adder takes five nanoseconds, and the clock period is ten.

7
00:00:38,483 --> 00:00:42,007
Follow one period, starting at a rising edge.

8
00:00:42,007 --> 00:00:47,163
One nanosecond later the register's output shows the stored total.

9
00:00:47,263 --> 00:00:51,469
Suppose the next number from the list shows up at that moment too.

10
00:00:51,469 --> 00:00:59,001
The adder now has both inputs, and five nanoseconds later, at six, the new sum is ready.

11
00:00:59,450 --> 00:01:06,457
The next edge comes at ten, and the register needs its input steady one nanosecond before that,

12
00:01:06,457 --> 00:01:07,638
so from nine on.

13
00:01:07,738 --> 00:01:13,432
The sum has been waiting since six, which leaves three nanoseconds to spare.

14
00:01:13,883 --> 00:01:17,939
In a real machine the two inputs rarely arrive together.

15
00:01:17,939 --> 00:01:22,294
Say the stored total shows up at one nanosecond as before,

16
00:01:22,294 --> 00:01:25,899
but the next number from the list only at three.

17
00:01:26,350 --> 00:01:31,508
For those two nanoseconds the adder sees the new total next to the old number.

18
00:01:31,508 --> 00:01:34,316
Right after the first number was stored,

19
00:01:34,316 --> 00:01:39,160
that means it starts adding three plus three, which nobody asked for.

20
00:01:39,616 --> 00:01:45,804
That wrong sum does travel through the adder, so for a while the output is garbage.

21
00:01:45,904 --> 00:01:50,789
Then at three the right number arrives, and five nanoseconds later,

22
00:01:50,789 --> 00:01:54,142
at eight, the output settles on the right sum.

23
00:01:54,583 --> 00:01:56,675
Eight is still before nine.

24
00:01:56,675 --> 00:02:03,885
The register only looks during its small window around the edge, so it never sees the garbage.

25
00:02:03,985 --> 00:02:06,449
This happens in every circuit.

26
00:02:06,449 --> 00:02:09,349
Signals wobble in the middle of a period,

27
00:02:09,349 --> 00:02:13,875
and all that matters is that they are quiet when the edge comes.

28
00:02:14,316 --> 00:02:16,722
So how short can the period be?

29
00:02:16,722 --> 00:02:23,308
Go back to both inputs arriving at one nanosecond, with the sum ready at six.

30
00:02:23,408 --> 00:02:25,280
Try a period of eight.

31
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:30,276
The window now opens at seven, and the sum is there in time.

32
00:02:30,716 --> 00:02:32,168
Try seven.

33
00:02:32,168 --> 00:02:38,504
The window opens at six, exactly when the sum settles, and that just works.

34
00:02:38,604 --> 00:02:40,356
Now try six.

35
00:02:40,356 --> 00:02:43,786
The window opens at five, the adder is still working,

36
00:02:43,786 --> 00:02:47,604
and the register stores whatever happens to be on the wire.

37
00:02:47,604 --> 00:02:52,454
The total is wrong, and so is every total after it.

38
00:02:52,916 --> 00:02:59,358
So seven nanoseconds is the shortest period, and we can read off where it comes from.

39
00:02:59,458 --> 00:03:04,051
One nanosecond for the register to show its value, five for the adder,

40
00:03:04,051 --> 00:03:08,446
and one for the setup time of the register that catches the result.

41
00:03:08,883 --> 00:03:13,605
In general the period has to be at least the clock to q delay,

42
00:03:13,605 --> 00:03:17,337
plus the delay of the logic, plus the setup time.

43
00:03:17,437 --> 00:03:23,834
The hold time is not in this sum, because it concerns what happens just after an edge,

44
00:03:23,834 --> 00:03:25,991
not how long the logic takes.

45
00:03:26,450 --> 00:03:32,820
A clock is usually described by its frequency, which is one divided by the period.

46
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:37,649
A period of seven nanoseconds is one seventh of a gigahertz,

47
00:03:37,649 --> 00:03:41,196
or about a hundred and forty three megahertz.

48
00:03:42,416 --> 00:03:46,516
Real circuits have many routes from one register to the next.

49
00:03:46,516 --> 00:03:52,736
Here is one from the course notes, with four AND gates that each take one nanosecond.

50
00:03:52,836 --> 00:03:58,053
The register has a clock to q delay and a setup time of one nanosecond each,

51
00:03:58,053 --> 00:04:03,202
and the four inputs on the left come from other registers of the same kind.

52
00:04:03,650 --> 00:04:09,160
Every route has to finish within one period, so the slowest route sets the pace.

53
00:04:09,160 --> 00:04:11,362
Let's time them.

54
00:04:11,462 --> 00:04:17,075
From this input the signal passes two gates before it reaches the register,

55
00:04:17,075 --> 00:04:19,920
and from these two it also passes two.

56
00:04:20,350 --> 00:04:25,678
But start from this input, or from the register's own output coming back around,

57
00:04:25,678 --> 00:04:29,808
and the signal passes this gate, then this one, then this one.

58
00:04:29,808 --> 00:04:32,160
That makes three.

59
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:39,006
No route goes through all four, because the lower gate sits beside that chain and not in it.

60
00:04:39,450 --> 00:04:42,474
The slowest route is called the critical path.

61
00:04:42,474 --> 00:04:50,260
Here it costs one nanosecond for clock to q, three for the gates and one for setup, which is five in all.

62
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:56,552
So the clock can run at one fifth of a gigahertz, and that is two hundred megahertz.

63
00:04:57,783 --> 00:05:02,281
The setup time gave us a rule that the logic must not be too slow.

64
00:05:02,281 --> 00:05:07,059
The hold time gives a second rule, and it points the other way.

65
00:05:07,159 --> 00:05:12,371
Take two registers on the same clock with almost nothing between them.

66
00:05:12,816 --> 00:05:16,814
At a rising edge the second register samples its input,

67
00:05:16,814 --> 00:05:21,466
and it needs that input to stay put until its hold time is over.

68
00:05:21,566 --> 00:05:27,498
But the same edge makes the first register change its output, one clock to q delay later.

69
00:05:27,498 --> 00:05:32,250
That change sets off down the wire toward the second register.

70
00:05:32,683 --> 00:05:39,933
If it gets there before the hold time is over, the second register's input moves inside the window.

71
00:05:40,033 --> 00:05:46,959
Say the hold time is two nanoseconds, clock to q is one, and the wire takes no time.

72
00:05:46,959 --> 00:05:51,333
The new value arrives at one, which is too early.

73
00:05:51,783 --> 00:05:55,891
So the second rule is that clock to q plus the fastest route

74
00:05:55,891 --> 00:05:59,247
through the logic must be at least the hold time.

75
00:05:59,347 --> 00:06:02,099
Notice that the clock period is not in it.

76
00:06:02,099 --> 00:06:08,061
Both events follow the same edge, so slowing the clock down does not help at all.

77
00:06:08,516 --> 00:06:11,290
What helps is to put some delay on the route.

78
00:06:11,290 --> 00:06:18,562
With one nanosecond of delay here, the new value arrives at two, just as the window closes.

79
00:06:18,662 --> 00:06:20,750
In practice this problem is rare,

80
00:06:20,750 --> 00:06:26,570
because flip-flops are usually built with a hold time no longer than their clock to q delay.

81
00:06:26,570 --> 00:06:30,152
Then even a bare wire is slow enough.

82
00:06:31,383 --> 00:06:36,979
So the clock period has a floor, set by the slowest route between two registers.

83
00:06:36,979 --> 00:06:42,053
And every route has a floor of its own, set by the hold time.

84
00:06:42,153 --> 00:06:47,867
With timing under control we can ask what a register in a loop is good for besides adding.

85
00:06:47,867 --> 00:06:50,757
Our circuit remembered a running total.

86
00:06:50,757 --> 00:06:54,291
What else could a circuit choose to remember?

